  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x² + 0.4 z²) / 2, drawn at half its value as height. x (left to right) is steep: plain descent multiplies x by 1 − 2.4·rate each step, so it overshoots above 0.42 and the swings grow above about 0.83.",
    "Each step moves by −rate × (slope + noise). The noise is random, centred on zero, with size 0.6·√((64−b)/(63b)) for batch b: largest at 1 and exactly zero at 64.",
    "Both arrows are steps (rate × slope estimate), drawn 8 times their true length, one scale for both. The purple arrow is the mini-batch step the ball takes next; the dark one is the full-data step.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or as diverged once the loss is above 4.8 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. Points off the bowl are drawn at its rim; the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The trail holds the start plus one point per step (at most 41). Noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence; each new run uses the next noise values.",
    "A single noisy step can raise the loss; it falls only on average. While running there are 5 updates per second at speed 1."
  ]
}